Q: PostgreSQL prepared statement becomes slower after repeated executions because the optimal plan changes A: <blockquote>
<p>How does PostgreSQL decide when to switch from a custom plan to a generic plan for a prepared statement</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Take a look at the <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/sql-prepare.html#SQL-PREPARE-NOTES:%7E:text=parameters%2E-,The,preferable" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><strong>Notes</strong> section in the <code>PREPARE</code> doc</a>:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The current rule for this is that the <strong>first five executions are done with custom plans and the average estimated cost of those plans is calculated. Then a generic plan is created and its estimated cost is compared to the average custom-plan cost. Subsequent executions use the generic plan if its cost is not so much higher than the average custom-plan cost as to make repeated replanning seem preferable.</strong></p>
<p>This heuristic can be overridden, forcing the server to use either generic or custom plans, by setting <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/runtime-config-query.html#GUC-PLAN-CACHE-MODE" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>plan_cache_mode</code></a> to <code>force_generic_plan</code> or <code>force_custom_plan</code> respectively.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Aside from checking your <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/runtime-config-query.html#GUC-PLAN-CACHE-MODE" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>plan_cache_mode</code></a> setting, you can take a look at the <code>.generic_plans</code> and <code>.custom_plans</code> counters in <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/view-pg-prepared-statements.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>pg_prepared_statements</code> system view</a> to see what you were dealing with.</p>
<p>There's at most a single <em>&quot;switch&quot;</em>, from custom to generic plan: if you're seeing <code>pg_prepared_statements.generic_plans</code> go up and <code>explain</code> shows <code>$</code> params, you'll have to <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/sql-deallocate.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>deallocate</code></a> and <code>prepare</code> it again to change it (a schema change, like altering a column type can also trigger a re-plan). The moment Postgres determines that re-using an already form generic plan is cheaper than custom ones are, plus their planning cost, it'll no longer try those, so they won't get a chance to drop in cost and win back the position.</p>
<p>The thing to note in <a href="https://github.com/postgres/postgres/blob/3d6a828938a5fa0444275d3d2f67b64ec3199eb7/src/backend/utils/cache/plancache.c#L1152" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>src/backend/utils/cache/plancache.c:1152</code></a> responsible for this is that <code>num_custom_plans</code> and <code>total_custom_cost</code> freeze the moment <code>generic_cost</code> wins, so from that point onward <code>generic_cost</code> will just keep winning.<br />
<sub>(edited for brevity)</sub></p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>static bool
choose_custom_plan(CachedPlanSource *plansource, ParamListInfo boundParams)
{
    double      avg_custom_cost;
    if (plansource-&gt;is_oneshot)/* One-shot plans will always be considered custom */
        return true;
    if (boundParams == NULL)/* Otherwise, never any point in a custom plan if there's no parameters */
        return false;
    if (!StmtPlanRequiresRevalidation(plansource))/* ... nor when planning would be a no-op */
        return false;
    /* Let settings force the decision */
    if (plan_cache_mode == PLAN_CACHE_MODE_FORCE_GENERIC_PLAN)
        return false;
    if (plan_cache_mode == PLAN_CACHE_MODE_FORCE_CUSTOM_PLAN)
        return true;
    /* See if caller wants to force the decision */
    if (plansource-&gt;cursor_options &amp; CURSOR_OPT_GENERIC_PLAN)
        return false;
    if (plansource-&gt;cursor_options &amp; CURSOR_OPT_CUSTOM_PLAN)
        return true;
    if (plansource-&gt;num_custom_plans &lt; 5)/* Generate custom plans until we have done at least 5 (arbitrary) */
        return true;
    avg_custom_cost = plansource-&gt;total_custom_cost / plansource-&gt;num_custom_plans;
    if (plansource-&gt;generic_cost &lt; avg_custom_cost)
        return false;
    return true;
}
</code></pre>
<p>They normally grow with each new custom plan, which simply stop getting spawned.</p>
<hr />
<blockquote>
<p>why can that make performance worse for skewed data</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Populate the table first, add indexing, consider adding <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/sql-createstatistics.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">extended stats</a>, then <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/sql-analyze.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>analyze</code></a> <em>before</em> allocating the prepared statement. Presence of data and an index on it doesn't mean <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/view-pg-stats.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>pg_stats</code></a> is already equipped to properly support the planner in its job, so <strong>you might potentially be freezing a suboptimal plan</strong>.</p>
<p>No amount of <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/sql-vacuum.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>vacuum</code></a>, <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/sql-analyze.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>analyze</code></a>, <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/sql-createindex.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>create index</code></a>, <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/sql-reindex.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>reindex</code></a>, <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/sql-cluster.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>cluster</code></a>, <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/sql-createstatistics.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>create statistics</code></a>, or <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/sql-altertable.html#SQL-ALTERTABLE-DESC-SET-STATISTICS" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>alter table..alter column..set statistics</code></a> that normally help the planner, will affect an already prepared statement, which already elected a generic plan - unless you use <code>force_custom_plan</code>, <code>auto</code> decided to stick to custom (re-)planning on its own, or some DDL triggered a re-plan the pushed it below <code>avg_custom_cost</code>.</p>
<p>The performance problem you saw doesn't really stem from the data being skewed but more likely from the plan being formulated and frozen at a time the skew wasn't yet clear to the planner. After all, the <strong>newer faster plan was made by that exact same planner</strong>, just using more recent and improved stats - which suggests that if you were to re-allocate the statement now, the inferior, generic plan would be unlikely to win.</p>
<p>If the internal planner can now come up with an improved custom plan, it might be able to come up with an improved <em>generic</em> plan as well, or at least make a better informed decision to stick to custom ones.</p>
<hr />
<h2>About the idea to move plan selection off the db:</h2>
<p>Just don't forget Postgres already has a planner built-in, and that's exactly its entire job that it can only do well if it's well-informed. If you're sure you can:</p>
<ol>
<li>Clearly define distinct variants of the same query and confirm they benefit from different plans.</li>
<li>Decide which one to use, faster than the planner can just make a fresh plan (~70μs in your MRE).</li>
<li>Define the rules behind that selection, and track what they depend on/their input better than the built-in <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/planner-stats.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">statistics system</a> and <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/runtime-config.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">cluster config</a>.</li>
<li>Confirm 1-3 above successfully outperform the built-in planner.</li>
<li>Confirm all 5 points here remain static: rules, required query variants and data profile aren't supposed to change.</li>
</ol>
<p>Then it <em>could</em> be worthwhile. It would still be a heavily simplified re-implementation of what the built-in planner is already capable of, but if you find it's easier to do that than to convince it to do it for you, then it's a perfectly viable workaround. In your example, that would mean allocating 2 separate statements, the &quot;rare&quot; one being the addition:</p>
<pre class="lang-sql prettyprint-override"><code>PREPARE get_events_account_1 (smallint) AS
SELECT id, created_at, amount
FROM invoice_events
WHERE account_id = 1
  AND event_type = $1
ORDER BY created_at DESC
LIMIT 20;
</code></pre>
<p>And you need to communicate if you're after the generic or the custom plan by switching <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/runtime-config-query.html#GUC-PLAN-CACHE-MODE" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>plan_cache_mode</code></a> accordingly. If you also use, or plan to use <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/plpgsql-implementation.html#PLPGSQL-PLAN-CACHING" rel="nofollow noreferrer">routines, they also cache plans</a>.</p>
<p>Most of your potential performance gains can get completely nullified not just by a major version upgrade, but even a minor one, or a slight shift in your data profile, a tiny addition to your business logic.</p>
<p>Developing and maintaining this sort of feature might very well turn out to require a much larger effort than migrating to a more recent version, or taking a moment to try alternative <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/ddl-partitioning.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">partitioning</a>, indexing and querying strategies, revisiting (<a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/79898518">de</a>-)<a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Database_normalization" rel="nofollow noreferrer">normalisation</a>, <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/manage-ag-tablespaces.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">tablespaces</a>, <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/storage-toast.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">compression and storage modes</a>, tuning the <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/runtime-config.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">cluster config</a> - obviously, all <em>after</em> making sure the methods of aiding the internal planner mentioned and linked earlier, are exhausted.</p>
<p>The built-in planner reacts to any of these naturally. A &quot;DIY plan picker&quot; would have to be subjected to constant re-evaluation and manual adjustment.</p>

Q: Is type qualifier on return type meaningless? A: <p>MSVC is wrong and the others are correct.
<code>const</code> has no effect in this case, but it is nevertheless part of the function's signature.</p>
<p>MSVC somehow forgets that the meaningless <code>const</code> is part of the signature when it comes to this assignment</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>const auto f(auto) { return 1; }
int(*x)(int) = f;
</code></pre>
<p>so it accepts it, while the other implementations rightfully rejects it.</p>
<p>MSVC is also inconsistent. It only seems to reject the assignment if the it's a function template with a deduced return type:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>const auto f(int) { return 1; }  // ok (only deduced return type)
const int f(auto) { return 1; }  // ok (only function template)
const auto f(auto) { return 1; } // NOK (both)
//
const int(*x)(int) = f;
</code></pre>
<p>It does however accept old-school template arguments:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>template&lt;class T&gt;
const auto f(T) { return 1; }
const int(*x)(int) = f; // ok by all implementations
</code></pre>

Q: How to replace images in a Word document using Office.js while preserving formatting and layout? A: <p>I had a similar issue, and what I ended up doing was to surround the inserted image with a content control, and then when replacing it I am able to get the formatting of the object so that I can make sure that when replacing I can set the same formatting.</p>
<p>This may or may not help you, but it is what worked for me ;)  (I have stripped out some code that is proprietary)</p>
<p>FYI, I am using a custom type to store information about any images I am adding to the document. One of the reasons for this is that I am displaying a &quot;copy&quot; of the image within the taskpane itself, so that when someone clicks on the image the cursor will jump to the document location where the image is.</p>
<p>See <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/76003058/track-ranges-across-different-word-run-contexts-using-officeextension-trackedobj">Track ranges across different Word.run contexts using OfficeExtension.TrackedObjects class</a> for more information.</p>
<pre><code>export interface OfficeImage{
  image: Word.InlinePicture | PowerPoint.Shape | Excel.Shape | undefined;
  base64Image: string;
  tag: string;
  title: string;
}
</code></pre>
<pre><code>public async replaceImage(
    oldImage: OfficeImage (custom type),
    newImage: OfficeImage,
  ): Promise&lt;void&gt; {
    const image = oldImage.image as Word.InlinePicture;

    await Word.run(image, async (context) =&gt; {
      const currentHeight = image.height;
      const currentWidth = image.width;
      image.untrack();
      await context.sync();

      await this.removeImage(oldImage);
      await this.insertImage(newImage, newImage.tag, {
        height: currentHeight,
        width: currentWidth,
      });
    });
  }
</code></pre>
<pre><code>public async insertImage(
    image: OfficeImage,
    tag: string,
    options?: { height: number; width: number },
  ) {
    await Word.run(async (context) =&gt; {
      const height = options?.height || 0;
      const width = options?.width || 0;

      const title = image.title || 'Untitled image';
      let range = context.document.getSelection();

      const parentControl = range.parentContentControlOrNullObject;
      await context.sync();
      if (!parentControl.isNullObject) {
        range = parentControl.getRange('After');
      }

      range.insertBreak('Line', 'Before');

      const image = range.insertInlinePictureFromBase64(image.base64Image, 'Replace');
      image.altTextTitle = title;
      image.altTextDescription = tag;
      if (height &gt; 0) {
        image.height = height;
      }
      if (width &gt; 0) {
        image.width = width;
      }

      image.track();
      addToList({ image, base64Image: image.base64Image, tag, title });
      range.select('End');
      await context.sync();
    });
  }
</code></pre>
